WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.200
There's kitsch, there's camp, and then there’s…
whatever is happening here.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:10.280
Even if you're someone who thinks the 1978
Star Wars Holiday Special is delightfully

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.180
bonkers, you gotta admit that there are a
few aspects that are... rather nightmarish.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.000
Agreed?

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.580
Before the opening credits, fans are treated
to a brief scene in which Han Solo promises

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:24.340
to get Chewbacca back home in time for Life
Day.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.380
"Uhhhhggggh."

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.780
"I know your family's waiting."

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:28.780
"UGHHH!"

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.100
"I know it's an important day!"

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.620
After we're dazzled by the star-studded main
title sequence, we get to meet Chewie's whole

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:36.120
family.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.160
And that's when the dread sets in.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.280
Sure, Wookiees are theoretically lovable…
but they're just really grating here.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.479
Suddenly, there's just a huge roomful of roaring
Wookiees - each with a different vocal pitch,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.080
and continually moaning for several minutes
straight.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.020
Is this not super-annoying to you?

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.400
The craziest part?

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.220
Their conversations aren't translated for
the audience.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.480
There are no subtitles whatsoever.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.620
It's a welcome relief when Chewie's rambunctious
son Lumpy settles down to watch some hologram

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.920
acrobats do their thing.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.800
But even after this bizarre spectacle, you
find yourself wondering, "What on Earth am

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:25.680
I watching?

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.180
What are they going on about?

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.900
Why are the dancers wearing sparkle-bikinis?"

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.220
It's very unsettling stuff on the best of
days.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.340
It's also really aggravating.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:44.510
Throughout the special, it's insanely obvious
that the young Star Wars stars aren't too

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.720
thrilled to be appearing in this special.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.140
Harrison Ford sounds particularly bored:

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.440
"That's it, I'm turning back."

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.520
Meanwhile, Mark Hamill - caked in what appears
to be several pounds of TV makeup - evidently

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.920
has no idea where he is, or what he's supposed
to be doing.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.200
At least he looks like he might actually be
receiving transmissions from space.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.000
"What's up??

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.200
Chewbacca…..?"

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.340
And Carrie Fisher - an actress who never shied
away from discussing her prolific drug use

00:02:12.349 --> 00:02:18.439
during this phase of her career - is clearly…
well, let's just say she's "coping" with the

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.200
production as best she can.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.640
"Mala, it's so good to see you…

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:23.900
Happy Life Day!"

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.599
Harvey Korman was a comedy treasure.

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:31.689
He was a staple of the '60s and '70s pop culture
landscape, thanks in no small part to his

00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:36.800
brilliant work on The Carol Burnett Show and
in classic Mel Brooks films like Blazing Saddles.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:42.120
His persnickety screen persona and impeccable
comedic timing made for so many unforgettable

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:42.620
moments.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.599
Unfortunately, none of these moments can be
found anywhere in The Star Wars Holiday Special.

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:53.700
In fact, Korman pops up not once, not twice,
but on three different occasions - and playing

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.319
three different characters, all of which we'll
get to in a hot minute.

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:01.480
Let's start with the first one: Gormaanda
is a television chef - and Chewie's wife Mala

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.220
clearly loves her cooking show.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.220
She watches Gormaanda intently as she prepares
the family's Life Day dinner.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.400
"Of course if your family has a hearty appetite,
I would suggest that old popular holiday favorite,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:14.680
the Bantha rump."

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.320
Gormaanda is evidently the science fiction
equivalent of Julia Child.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.180
It's a premise that's not without comedic
potential, but there's just something about

00:03:23.189 --> 00:03:26.620
the frenzied, manic energy that we find deeply
unnerving.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:27.709
"Stir, whip!

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:28.799
Stir, whip!

00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:30.989
Whip, whip, whip, stir!

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:33.900
Stir, whip, stir, whip, whip whip…"

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.060
Sorry, Gormaanda.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.540
You and your four spider-like arms seem to
mean well… but you're just a bit much for

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:40.920
our delicate sensibilities.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.600
No disrespect to the late Diahann Carroll,
but exactly how did this cameo come about?

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.920
Perhaps she thought a Star Wars special sounded
like a plum gig, and she certainly did the

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.540
best she could with what she was given.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.720
It's just that what she was given, well, there's
really no way to put it delicately.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.800
As you can see — and as you can never unsee
— she depicts the holographic object of

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.380
unbridled Wookiee lust.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.540
Mmm… is it getting hot in here?

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.579
Chewie's father - nickname of Itchy - is enjoying
what's called a "mind evaporator."

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:17.340
This handy gadget is perhaps responsible for
The Star Wars Holiday Special's most uncomfortable

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:17.840
moment.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.220
The Wookiees' family friend Saun - played
by Honeymooners star Art Carney of all people

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.700
- gives Itchy a very special Life Day gift:

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:26.060
"It's kinda hard to explain.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:26.560
It's a...

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.860
WOW.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.280
Ya know what I mean??"

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.420
It takes awhile for her to come into focus,
but suddenly, there she is: the legendary

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:40.620
Diahann Carroll, playing some sort of intergalactic
disco nymph, much to the delight of the dirty

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.220
old Wookiee.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.580
"Oh, yes.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.600
I can feel my creation."

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:50.800
"I'm getting your message."

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:52.960
Yeah, we're all getting the message.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.260
After her coyly seductive introduction, she
launches into a performance of the song "This

00:04:58.260 --> 00:04:58.760
Minute."

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.820
It's a perfectly lovely tune, if perhaps very,
very, very, very long.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.360
And Itchy makes the whole thing feel, well,
itchy.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:12.060
The way he grunts, and the way those rancid
gums flap excitedly!

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:13.180
It's all a bit much.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.840
"We can have a good time.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:17.120
Can we?"

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:19.580
"Mmm."

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:24.100
And in case you think any of this is accidental,
we should mention that the shooting script

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.980
specifically indicates that:

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.600
"[Saun presents his gift] as if he is giving
Itchy the most X-rated piece of literature

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:31.610
in the galaxy."

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:36.870
Much like Gormaanda, the second character
played by Harvey Korman is also part of an

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:37.880
instructional video.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:43.340
Dromboid is a not-so plucky robot who appears
on screen to teach a certain Lumpy - hi, Lumpy

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.740
- how to assemble a mini-transmitter.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.280
So: Is Dromboid's scene better or worse than
Gormaanda's?

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:50.860
Well... it's different.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.720
Very, very different.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.270
The "joke" in this sequence - if you want
to be generous and call it a joke - is that

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:02.440
Dromboid has a tendency to break down, slurring
his speech and occasionally unable to move.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.520
"But now let's get started, shall we?"

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.100
Sometimes he talks very slowly or stops to
adjust various robot parts.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:13.080
That's it.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:14.600
That's the whole thing.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:15.980
And it goes on for several minutes.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:16.980
The worst part?

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:20.500
Dromboid's appearance occurs about two-thirds
of the way through the special.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:24.240
And by that point, you're already starting
to get concerned that this thing is never

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.240
going to end.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.850
And then suddenly, you're asked to deal with
this kind of material:

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:34.720
"And we'll assemble our mini-transmitters
together.”

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:40.520
In one of the special's many awkward scenes,
we find ourselves at the Mos Eisley cantina...

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:44.370
the place where Luke Skywalker and Obi-Wan
Kenobi met Han Solo and Chewbacca for the

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:45.040
first time.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.000
And this is where we eventually meet Krelman
the third and final Harvey Korman character.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.680
Before you can say "Maclunkey!," he's already
making your skin scrawl.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:58.740
Krelman is chatting up the unflappable bartender
Ackmena - played by the inimitable Bea Arthur

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.240
- in what is clearly an ongoing one-sided
flirtation.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.140
It's played like an old-fashioned comedy routine,
with two charming performers and the special's

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:08.080
sharpest writing.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:13.020
Ackmena's prickly dialogue is genuinely funny,
especially with the laser-blast precision

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.300
of Bea Arthur's delivery.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:19.540
Unfortunately, none of that can save the scene
from the icky feeling of watching a "Nice

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:20.990
Guy" refuse to take a hint.

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:22.880
"Oh excuse me I have a customer waiting.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:23.580
I'll be right back."

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:25.860
"Come back soon.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:27.200
I'll be waiting."

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.150
Look at poor Ackmena, bound by professional
courtesy to put up with him - if perhaps none

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:34.980
too politely.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.160
It's all just way too real.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:43.200
"I just came in here for a drink… and I
found a lot more than that."

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:44.300
"Uh, Curlman…"

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:44.980
"Krelman."

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:49.480
There's also a bit of a physical ick factor
in the way Krelman pours a drink into a hole

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:50.660
in the top of his head.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:54.460
Is that a second mouth up there that's just
always open?

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:55.720
Weird, dude.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:01.100
The good times at the cantina come to an abrupt
end following a major buzzkill of an announcement.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:02.220
Oh no…

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:03.000
Not on Life Day …

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:09.860
"Due to increased activity among subversive
forces, we are placing a curfew on the entire

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:11.360
Tatooine system effective immediately."

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.980
That means Ackmena has to usher all her loyal
patrons out - possibly forever.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:19.340
Getting the alien revelers to leave proves
to be a challenge.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.420
"FELLAS, THE PARTY IS OVER!!"

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.720
And so she bursts into a rousing number called
"Goodnight, But Not Goodbye.”

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:30.860
It's a song that would get just about anyone
out the door in a jiffy.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:36.620
The whole thing is, of course, deeply silly,
but it also turns out to be surprisingly heartfelt.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:41.900
Bea Arthur, as always, gives it her all - stridently
pleading to a room full of rubber-headed monster

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:42.560
masks.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:46.500
Is it just us, or does the whole scene end
up rather affecting in the end?

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.120
Really, it all feels a bit like Casablanca,
but with a Hammerhead.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:54.680
And what exactly is the relationship between
Ackmena and this humanoid rat?

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:56.760
They're awfully tender with one another.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:57.440
Anyone?

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:58.260
Thoughts?

00:08:58.460 --> 00:08:58.960
Hello?

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.280
Star Wars has always seemed to riff off some
real-world conflicts.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.800
Fortunately, the films are so fast-paced,
fantastical, and full of space wizards with

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.340
laser swords, you never get a chance to start
dwelling on our real issues here on Earth.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:17.370
But the stakes get a little too real in The
Star Wars Holiday Special, especially since

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:20.320
it's presumably geared towards undiscerning
children.

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:25.040
There's the aforementioned scene in which
authorities clamp down on so-called "subversive

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:25.540
activity."

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:30.300
And then there's this scene, in which a stormtrooper
tears apart a young Wookiees' bedroom in search

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.200
of hidden rebels.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:32.500
Happy holidays!

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:43.900
In fact, The Empire has never felt more like
a Nazi regime than they do here.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:48.050
The special pays a lot of attention to the
domestic lives of civilians: You see them

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:52.300
preparing for a joyous holiday celebration,
but the festivities occur beneath the looming

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.240
shadow of encroaching fascism.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.740
It's bizarrely heavy-handed stuff for a holiday
special, no?

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:03.360
As The Star Wars Holiday Special approaches
its climax, Chewie arrives home with an intensely

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:08.880
bored-looking Han Solo, who basically manages
to trip a stormtrooper, to that stormtrooper's

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:09.380
death!

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.000
Like, what just happened here?

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:16.500
At last reunited with his desperately annoying
family, Chewbacca begins preparations for

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:17.120
Life Day.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:22.160
Apparently, this highly anticipated event
involves everyone grabbing whatever orbs happen

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:27.200
to be lying around, and then marching slowly
and determinedly in their red cloaks towards

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:28.730
a large open space.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:30.720
Then, they just sort of stand there.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:35.640
Finally, Princess Leia bursts into song - a
Life Day carol titled "A Day to Celebrate."

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:39.420
And it just so happens to be set to the tune
of the main Star Wars theme.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:40.860
Oh, the pageantry!

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:45.080
This all begs the question: What on Earth
is Life Day?

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.260
In the intro to her song, Leia mentions:

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:49.540
"This is the promise of the Tree of Life."

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.720
Hmm… first we ever heard anything about
this Tree of Life.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.780
Presumably this is a huge trunk in the background,
right?

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:58.060
Is this tree trunk part of the Tree of Life?

00:10:58.200 --> 00:10:59.560
We may never know.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:04.560
Then there's a druggy montage of random Star
Wars scenes, as everyone stands around, presumably

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:06.980
meditating on just how rad Star Wars is.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.480
Maybe that's the true meaning of Life Day:
Star Wars is rad.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:16.520
The winter of 1978 was only a few months removed
from the record-blasting box office run of

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:22.220
Star Wars: Many theaters kept the movie playing
for well over a year past its May 1977 debut.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.880
With The Empire Strikes Back still a year
and a half away, the prospect of a Star Wars

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:28.760
holiday special was obviously a big deal.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:33.580
Just imagine the thrill of hearing an announcer
finally rattle off the names of all your favorite

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:34.640
Star Wars characters.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:35.740
And then, this happened:

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.500
"R2-D2 as R2-D2!"

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:42.140
That one's a bit of a head-scratcher, isn't
it?

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:47.420
As true adherents of the Force know, R2-D2
was originally performed by the late Kenny

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:52.060
Baker, who controlled the droid in both the
original and prequel trilogies - and usually

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:53.399
from inside the costume.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.320
There are conflicting reports as to whether
Baker was actually involved in the holiday

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:02.550
special: Sometimes he's credited with working
on the 1978 spectacle, but it's worth noting

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:07.730
that filmmaker Mick Garris ists remote-controlling
R2-D2 for the holiday special as one of his

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:08.940
earliest gigs.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:14.190
Regardless, claiming R2 simply played himself
is a bit insulting to the performers involved,

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:18.240
and it suggests the droid somehow went sentient
for this cheesy variety show.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.160
Of course, there's also the distinct possibility
that no one wanted to take credit for R2's

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:23.940
performance here.

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:29.540
The Star Wars franchise is, of course, a monster
success, which makes the Star Wars Holiday

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:31.440
Special very special, indeed.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:35.980
And though the films all have a timeless quality
about them, the holiday special is very much

00:12:35.980 --> 00:12:37.100
a product of the '70s.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:41.020
In fact, it looks like your screen has been
lightly smeared in Vaseline.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:46.920
But where else can you find R2-D2 existing
alongside Jefferson Starship playing themselves,

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:49.760
and also playing one certifiable clunker of
a song.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:55.880
The Star Wars Holiday Special is unique, surprising,
woefully misguided, and an absolute joy to

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:56.380
watch.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:01.480
And yes, it's disturbing, unless snuggling
up to drunken space-rats is your idea of a

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:01.980
good time.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:03.520
Then again, who are we to judge?

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:04.720
Love is love.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.490
"I find you adorable."

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:09.940
"I'll tell you a secret.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:12.300
I find you adorable."

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.600
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:20.280
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:24.540
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

